---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import ConcreteCalculator from '@components/islands/ConcreteCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'concrete-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How much concrete do I need?', answer: 'Multiply the length, width and depth of your slab to get the volume, then convert to cubic yards. In feet, the shortcut is length × width × thickness ÷ 27, because there are 27 cubic feet in a cubic yard. Enter your dimensions above and the calculator does it — including the number of pre-mix bags.' },
  { question: 'How do I estimate concrete in cubic yards?', answer: 'Convert every measurement to feet first (a 4-inch slab is 4 ÷ 12 = 0.33 ft), multiply length × width × thickness for cubic feet, then divide by 27 for cubic yards. A 10 × 10 ft pad at 4 inches is 10 × 10 × 0.33 ÷ 27 ≈ 1.24 cubic yards.' },
  { question: 'How many bags of concrete are in a cubic yard?', answer: 'Roughly 45 × 80-lb bags, 60 × 60-lb bags, or 90 × 40-lb bags make one cubic yard. Because that is a lot of mixing, anything beyond about a cubic yard is usually cheaper and easier as ready-mix delivery.' },
  { question: 'How much does a cubic yard of concrete cost?', answer: 'This calculator estimates volume and bag counts, not cost. To budget, take the cubic yards it returns and multiply by your local price: ready-mix commonly runs in the region of $125–$175 per cubic yard delivered, though prices vary widely by supplier, mix and delivery conditions, and small loads carry a short-load fee. Bagged mix costs more per yard but suits jobs too small to order a truck.' },
  { question: 'How long does concrete take to dry and cure?', answer: 'You can usually walk on new concrete after 24–48 hours and drive on it after about 7 days, but it keeps hardening for weeks — reaching most of its rated strength at 28 days. Keeping it damp during that period (curing) makes the finished slab stronger.' },
  { question: 'Why add a waste allowance?', answer: 'Some concrete is always lost to spillage, uneven subgrade and over-excavation. Adding about 5–10% extra ensures you do not run short mid-pour, which is far more costly than a little leftover.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" intro="Estimate how much concrete a slab or footing needs — in cubic yards, cubic metres and pre-mix bags — with an optional waste allowance." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-25' }}>
  <ConcreteCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the <strong>length</strong>, <strong>width</strong> and <strong>depth</strong> of your slab or footing.</li>
      <li>Choose the <strong>unit</strong> — feet or metres. Switching it converts the three dimensions you have already typed rather than reinterpreting them.</li>
      <li>Optionally add a <strong>waste allowance</strong> as a percentage (leave it blank for none).</li>
      <li>Read the result in <strong>cubic yards</strong>, with cubic-metre and cubic-foot equivalents and the number of 40, 60 and 80-lb bags.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      All three dimensions use the same unit, so a slab thickness quoted in inches has to be
      converted first: divide inches by 12 to get feet, which makes a 4-inch pour 0.333 ft.
      Switch the unit to metres if your measurements are metric.
    </p>

    <h2>How concrete estimating works</h2>
    <p>
      Concrete is sold by volume, so every estimate comes down to turning a slab's dimensions
      into cubic yards. Multiply length by width by depth for the volume in cubic feet, then
      divide by 27 — there are 27 cubic feet in a cubic yard. The bag counts come from dividing
      that volume by how much each pre-mix bag yields once mixed.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>For dimensions measured in feet:</p>
    <p><strong>cubic yards = (length × width × depth) ÷ 27</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>length × width × depth</strong> — the volume in cubic feet, with depth in feet (a 4-inch slab is 0.333 ft).</li>
      <li><strong>÷ 27</strong> — converts cubic feet to cubic yards, the unit ready-mix is ordered in.</li>
      <li>A <strong>waste allowance</strong>, if entered, multiplies the volume by 1 + the percentage before the bag counts are worked out.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Choose metres instead and the tool converts the dimensions to feet first, so the method
      and the 27-cubic-feet-per-yard constant stay the same underneath.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Say you are pouring a <strong>12 ft × 12 ft patio, 4 inches thick</strong>. Four inches
      is one-third of a foot, so the volume is 12 × 12 × 0.333 = <strong>48 cubic feet</strong>.
      Divide by 27 and that is <strong>1.78 cubic yards</strong>, or about <strong>1.36 cubic
      metres</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      As bags, 48 cubic feet works out to <strong>80 × 80-lb bags</strong> (about 107 × 60-lb
      or 160 × 40-lb) — a lot of hauling and mixing, which is exactly why a pour this size is
      usually ordered as ready-mix. Add a 10% waste allowance and the order rises to roughly
      <strong>1.96 cubic yards</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Bags or ready-mix?</h2>
    <p>Bagged pre-mix suits small jobs — footings, fence posts, a single small pad. Each bag covers only a fraction of a cubic foot, so the count climbs fast:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Bag size</th><th>Yield per bag</th><th>Bags per cubic yard</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>40 lb</td><td>~0.30 cu ft</td><td>~90</td></tr>
        <tr><td>60 lb</td><td>~0.45 cu ft</td><td>~60</td></tr>
        <tr><td>80 lb</td><td>~0.60 cu ft</td><td>~45</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For anything much over a cubic yard, ordering ready-mix by the yard is usually cheaper and far less work than mixing dozens of bags by hand.</p>

    <h2>Why add a waste allowance</h2>
    <p>
      Some concrete is always lost to spillage, an uneven subgrade and slight over-excavation,
      and running short mid-pour is far more costly than a little leftover. A margin of about
      <strong>5–10%</strong> covers it on a typical slab; add more for a rough or sloping site.
      The bag counts above already include whatever percentage you enter.
    </p>

    <h2>How long concrete takes to set and cure</h2>
    <p>Fresh concrete is firm enough to walk on within 24–48 hours and strong enough to drive on after about a week, but it keeps gaining strength for weeks, reaching most of its rated value at <strong>28 days</strong>. Keeping the surface damp through the first days — curing — measurably increases the finished strength.</p>
    <p>These are estimates; confirm quantities with your supplier and allow for your own site conditions. For a room's floor area rather than a pour, see the <a href="/everyday/square-footage-calculator">square footage calculator</a>, or the <a href="/math/volume-calculator">volume calculator</a> for cylinders, columns and other shapes.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
